  "query_preview": "..An upper level disturbance is moving northwest into\neast central Arizona now and then into north central Arizona \ntonight. While there was a slower start to convection over much of\nour area today, the deep moisture and instability in place is\nallowing numerous storms to develop. The greatest coverage is over\neastern Arizona, spreading westward and impact the rest of \nnorthern Arizona later in the afternoon and evening. The primary \nthreat continues to be heavy rainfall and flash flooding, \nespecially considering the wet ground conditions and many \nstreams/rivers are already flowing and elevated. For this reason, \nthere is a Flash Flood Watch in effect through late this evening \nfor all of our forecast area excluding the far northeast corner. \nThere continues to be a localized strong wind/hail threat today, \nespecially over northeast Arizona through the early evening. As \nthe disturbance moves northward Wednesday, scattered thunderstorms\nare expected in the afternoon, especially over the higher terrain\nwith a somewhat lower threat of heavy rainfall. From Thursday \ninto the weekend, the center of high pressure to our east is \nforecast to shift toward Arizona. While moisture levels will drop,\nthere will still be enough for daily scattered storms over the \nhigher terrain, but more isolated for the valleys. Temperatures \nwill also begin to heat up to above normal levels. Temperatures by\nthe weekend may approach warning levels for the lowest elevations\nof the Grand Canyon so we'll keep an eye on that.",
